        'Castle builders added clever defensive features. A moat, a deep ditch often filled with water, surrounded many castles, and a drawbridge could be raised to block the entrance. Behind the gate, a heavy iron grille called a portcullis could also be lowered. Narrow arrow slits in the walls were thin on the outside but wide on the inside, allowing guards to see out while remaining well protected. Daily life inside, however, was far from luxurious: stone rooms were cold and draughty, and the household often ate together in a large room called the great hall, warmed by a central fire.',
