        'Every device connected to the internet has a numerical label called an IP address. Because long strings of numbers are difficult for people to remember, the Domain Name System, or DNS, works like an enormous contacts list, translating familiar website names into the IP addresses that computers actually use. The older addressing system, IPv4, allows only about 4.3 billion unique addresses, which is not enough for every device on Earth, so a newer system called IPv6 is gradually replacing it. Specialised devices called routers then examine each packet\'s destination and pass it along, step by step, towards its target. Remarkably, packets from the same file may travel along entirely different routes.',
